<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>dev.olivelli.musicwizard</groupId>
    <artifactId>music-wizard</artifactId>
    <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>mw-notation</artifactId>
  <name>Music Wizard :: Notation</name>
  <description>Score to LilyPond, MusicXML, MIDI and PDF. Must not depend on mw-ml.</description>

  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
      <artifactId>mw-core</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <!--
      The quantizer's per-bar grid decision, which cannot be recovered from the
      positions it produced: three notes a third of a beat apart and three a
      half beat apart are both legal on the sixth-of-a-beat grid, and only one
      of them is a triplet. Symbolic only, and mw-arrange depends on nothing but
      mw-core, so this does not pull audio or models into the notation layer.
      See #92.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
      <artifactId>mw-arrange</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.audiveris</groupId>
      <artifactId>proxymusic</artifactId>
    </dependency>  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <!--
            A hostile message locale for the test JVM, so that the check on
            LilyPondRenderer wiring cannot pass by accident.

            That test starts a stand-in binary and asserts it was handed
            LC_MESSAGES=C. On a machine whose own LC_MESSAGES is already C (a
            bare container, or a CI file that pins it after this very thread)
            the child sees C whether or not the code under test ran, and round 8
            of review found that deleting both call sites left the suite green
            there. It is the third time on this branch that an assertion was
            being satisfied by the parent environment rather than by the code.

            Pinned here rather than left to the machine, so the poison is part
            of the build and not a property of somebody's shell.

            POSIX, and the value matters as much as the variable. The first
            version of this said it_IT.UTF-8 on the reasoning that LC_MESSAGES
            selects a message catalogue and nothing else. Round 9 of review
            measured the forked JVM and found sun.jnu.encoding collapsed to
            ANSI_X3.4-1968, because glibc's locale selection is all or nothing
            and it_IT.UTF-8 is not installed on most machines. That is
            the same mechanism speakEnglish exists to work around, one process
            out, and it made mw-notation the one module unable to hold a
            non-ASCII file name, in the branch whose subject is that they must
            keep engraving.

            So the value has to be one that always resolves, and has to differ
            from C so the assertion in LilyPondRendererTest can tell the two
            apart. POSIX is both.
          -->
          <environmentVariables>
            <LC_MESSAGES>POSIX</LC_MESSAGES>
          </environmentVariables>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
